WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:04.875
LACE–CRAFT reuses learned control to refine robot bodies and rewards.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.768
Each round starts with the best robot so far.

3
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.584
The first route continues training, restoring the saved policy and optimizer state.

4
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.920
The second route adapts new designs. It copies the policy and initializes a fresh optimizer.

5
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:26.528
To propose the changes, CRAFT examines the replay and its recorded trajectory.

6
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.776
Feedback records where the robot loses balance.

7
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:37.408
Morphology and Reward use that feedback to develop body and reward changes together.

8
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.792
They cross-review the proposals and write the revisions on the blackboard.

9
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.456
Integration pairs the reviewed body and reward changes.

10
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.400
The resulting body and reward combinations become new candidates.

11
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.840
They join the continued robot and the best robot so far in the candidate pool.

12
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.664
The continued and new designs receive the same additional training budget.

13
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.480
The trained controller is tested in a new rollout.

14
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.528
LACE selects the best candidate.

15
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.136
The new replay and its full trajectory are saved for the next round.

16
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.960
Across five tasks, the selected policies score higher than D2C under the reported budgets.

17
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:32.130
Each fixed policy is evaluated with three seeds and one hundred twenty-eight episodes per seed.

18
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.620
Removing actor inheritance or replay evidence lowers the mean score on every task.

19
00:01:45.447 --> 00:01:50.619
A reference image guides the body shape, and Rodin produces the initial mesh.

20
00:01:51.442 --> 00:01:57.499
BANG separates the components. Scripts repair and align them into editable body and limb meshes.

21
00:01:57.705 --> 00:02:03.163
Configured joint axes and limits define how the body and limbs articulate.

22
00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:11.108
Motor cavities and mating surfaces provide the interfaces. The torso also carries a mount for the existing SO-101 arm.

23
00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:18.338
The hardware-adapted robot trains walking with twelve leg joints and a fixed arm.

24
00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:26.140
Hardware adaptation refits the torso, leg parts, and motor housings for printing.

25
00:02:27.142 --> 00:02:30.193
The robot is assembled with an SO-101 arm.

26
00:02:30.632 --> 00:02:35.096
Because of the current hardware constraints, walking is tested with the arm removed.

27
00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:37.757
LACE reuses learned control.

28
00:02:38.081 --> 00:02:40.104
CRAFT reviews body and reward changes.

29
00:02:40.427 --> 00:02:42.683
The workflow extends to hardware prototyping.
